---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ESSAYS, HOW_PATH, SITE_LAB, adrUrl, essayUrl, howUrl, labUrl } from '../../lib/links';

// The essay the Queue Lab maps to (docs/domain.md, "The principle map"; Q33 · Q-principles). Static,
// no island. Every claim about Cloudflare's behaviour is as its documentation stated it on the
// date in READ, and every number from the code names its file. What the lab shows is the lab page
// as read that day.
const READ = '2026-11-06';
const { title } = ESSAYS.queue;
const SOURCES = [
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/reference/delivery-guarantees/',
    title: 'Delivery guarantees',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Queues documentation',
    for: 'at least once by default, rarely more than once, and a unique id per message as the idempotency key',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/configuration/batching-retries/',
    title: 'Batching, Retries and Delays',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Queues documentation',
    for: 'ack and retry per message, three retries by default, batches of up to 100',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/configuration/dead-letter-queues/',
    title: 'Dead Letter Queues',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Queues documentation',
    for: 'a message moves to the dead-letter queue at the retry limit, and without one it is deleted permanently',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/platform/pricing/',
    title: 'Queues pricing',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Queues documentation',
    for: 'three operations to deliver a message, a read for each retry, 10,000 a day on Free and 1,000,000 a month on Paid, then $0.40 a million',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://microservices.io/patterns/data/transactional-outbox.html',
    title: 'Pattern: Transactional outbox',
    by: 'microservices.io, after the book Microservices Patterns (2018)',
    for: 'the message stored in the same transaction as the change, and a relay that might publish it more than once',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://www.enterpriseintegrationpatterns.com/patterns/messaging/IdempotentReceiver.html',
    title: 'Idempotent Receiver',
    by: 'Enterprise Integration Patterns (book, 2003), and its site',
    for: 'a receiver that can safely receive the same message several times',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://www.enterpriseintegrationpatterns.com/patterns/messaging/DeadLetterChannel.html',
    title: 'Dead Letter Channel',
    by: 'Enterprise Integration Patterns (book, 2003), and its site',
    for: 'where a messaging system puts a message it cannot deliver',
  },
  {
    href: labUrl('Queue Lab') as string,
    title: 'Crash the workers behind a queue',
    by: 'the Queue Lab on ihaveasegway.com',
    for: 'workers crashed between the work and the acknowledgement, an idempotency switch and a dead-letter queue, simulated in your browser',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={`${title} | Proof of Concept Bakery`}
  description="How Proof of Concept Bakery gets a paid order to the kitchen, the CRM and the inbox with no transaction between them: the outbox, a queue that delivers at least once, receivers that remember, and the dead letters."
>
  <article class="essay prose">
    <p class="hint">
      <a href={HOW_PATH}>How it’s built</a>
    </p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      When a visitor pays for an order, six other objects have to hear about it, and none of them
      can share a transaction with the order. This is how each hears once in effect, though the
      message may travel twice, what happens when it cannot travel, and where the retrying stops.
    </p>

    <h2>A paid order, and who has to hear</h2>
    <p>
      Two cardamom buns and a country loaf, paid by test card. Stripe’s webhook reaches the Billing
      object, which records <code>PaymentSucceeded</code>; that event reaches the Order, which
      records <code>OrderPaid</code>. As built on {READ}, the routing table in
      <code>crates/app/src/routing.rs</code> sends <code>OrderPaid</code> to six subscribers: the
      day’s Schedule, which turns the hold into a reservation; the Kitchen, which moves the card to
      confirmed; the Crm; the order’s Customer; the Inbox, which writes the receipt; and the day’s
      Dispatch, which confirms a delivery’s stop.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each is a Durable Object with storage of its own, and{' '}
      <a href={essayUrl('raft')}>no transaction spans two objects</a>. So the Order cannot record
      the payment and move the card in one step. It writes its own state and then tells the others,
      and everything below is about that word “then”.
    </p>

    <h2>What the Queue Lab shows, and what it cannot</h2>
    <p>
      The{' '}
      <a href={labUrl('Queue Lab')} rel="noopener">
        Queue Lab
      </a>
      , as read on {READ}, has three workers take orders from a broker and print oven tickets. Crash
      one after it prints and before it acknowledges, and another worker prints the same order
      again. Its page concludes that a queue “delivers at least once. It cannot promise exactly
      once”, and exactly once “is something the worker builds on top”, with a record of the ids
      already printed. Orders that keep failing go to a dead-letter queue after four tries.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two things are outside it. It starts at the broker, so getting a message there with the write
      that caused it is not drawn. And it says it writes the record and the ticket “together, in one
      atomic step”, which a real printer could not join. In the bakery the first is the hard part,
      and the second is true: every ticket is a row.
    </p>

    <h2>Two steps, and the gap between them</h2>
    <p>
      The Order’s write and the send to the Queue are two operations on two systems, with a moment
      between them whichever comes first. Send first and fail before the commit, and the board shows
      a paid card for an unpaid order. Commit first and fail before the send, and the kitchen never
      hears.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bakery commits first and treats the send as something that may happen more than once. In
      one synchronous transaction (<code>workers/api/src/objects/stream.rs</code>) the Order appends{' '}
      <code>OrderPaid</code> to its <code>events</code> table, copies the envelope into its
      <code>outbox</code> table, writes its new state and records the command’s key. That is the
      transactional outbox pattern: the message is stored with the change, so it exists exactly when
      the change does. After the commit the object sets an outbox timer 30 seconds out, arms its
      alarm for it, and, while the visitor already has the answer, sends up to 100 outbox rows to
      the
      <code>bakery-events</code> Queue in one batch, deleting the rows the Queue accepted.
    </p>
    <p>
      The gap is still there, but the only failure left in it is a duplicate. If the isolate stops
      after the Queue took the batch and before the delete, the rows go again. If two commands reach
      one object in quick succession, the second send can read rows the first has not yet deleted.
      The pattern’s page says as much: the relay “might publish a message more than once”.
    </p>

    <h2>What happens to a row the Queue refused</h2>
    <p>
      As the code reads on {READ}: when the batch send fails, every row stays, each row’s
      <code>attempts</code> count goes up by one (nothing reads it yet), and the object logs
      <code>relay_error</code>. Two things send the rows again. The outbox timer fires 30 seconds
      after the commit, and the alarm sends what is left, setting the timer another 30 seconds out
      while rows remain. And the object’s next command sends the whole outbox after its own commit,
      oldest row first. The 15-minute cron is not involved: it retries unfinished wipes and never
      reads an outbox.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each alarm run that arms the alarm again is counted by the loop guard,
      <code>REARM_GUARD</code> in <code>stream.rs</code>: 100 a UTC day for one object. At one every
      30 seconds, a Queue that refuses everything for 50 minutes uses up the day; the 101st re-arm
      is refused and logged <code>alarm_guard</code>, and the rows wait for the object’s next
      command, which may always arm, or for the wipe at 10:00 UTC. Past the guard a row waits; it is
      never dropped.
    </p>

    <h2>At least once, applied once</h2>
    <p>
      Cloudflare’s Queues documentation, read on {READ}, promises delivery at least once and rarely
      more, and recommends a unique id per message as the key to drop repeats. The bakery adds
      repeats of its own: its consumer, <code>workers/api/src/queue.rs</code>, acknowledges a
      message only when every subscriber answered 2xx, so when the Kitchen fails,
      <code>OrderPaid</code> goes again to the five that already applied it.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the receiving end remembers: <em>Enterprise Integration Patterns</em>’ idempotent receiver.
      Each subscriber stores an applied event’s id in the same write as the change: the Schedule and
      the Dispatch in a <code>seen</code> table, the Kitchen in a set inside the board’s one row,
      the Order in its
      <code>commands_seen</code>. A second delivery finds the id and changes nothing. At-least-once
      delivery and a receiver that remembers is the only exactly once there is, and it holds here
      for the reason the lab gives as its simplification: every effect the bakery has is a write to
      an object it owns, or a call to Stripe with the order’s id as the key.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the retries stop</h2>
    <p>
      The consumer takes batches of up to 10 and allows 3 retries, with the dead-letter queue
      <code>bakery-events-dlq</code> (<code>workers/api/wrangler.jsonc</code>). Cloudflare’s page on
      dead-letter queues, read on {READ}, says a message at the retry limit with no such queue is
      deleted permanently. The bakery’s is read by the same handler, which writes each message, with
      the last error recorded for it, into the owning Sandbox’s <code>dead_letters</code>, keyed by
      the event’s id, until a redrive or the wipe at 10:00 UTC.
    </p>
    <p>
      A redrive sends the stored envelope to the Queue again with the same id and deletes the row
      after the send, so a failure between the two can send it twice and never lose it, and the
      subscribers’ records make the repeat harmless.
    </p>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Three Queue operations an event.</strong> Cloudflare’s pricing page, read on {READ}:
        a write, a read and a delete per message, and a read per retry. The bakery’s cost model (
        <code>docs/cost.md</code>, as of 2026-11-04) assumes 50 events a session, 150 operations,
        against 10,000 a day on Free and 1,000,000 a month on Paid, then $0.40 a million.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Twelve for an event that dead-letters and is redriven,</strong> by the same count: 3
        and 3 retries on <code>bakery-events</code>, 3 on the dead-letter queue, 3 for the redrive.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An alarm run for every commit that wrote events.</strong> The timer is set whether
        or not the first send worked, so 30 seconds later the alarm finds an empty outbox and
        deletes it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A person.</strong> Until someone fixes the cause and redrives a dead letter, the
        kitchen does not have that card.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Sign in, open <a href="/app/">the console</a>, and under Faults tick “Kitchen refuses
      deliveries”. Order from <a href="/menu/">the menu</a> and pay with the test card. The order is
      paid, and after three retries the Dead letters list holds the Kitchen’s events, each with the
      error “Kitchen answered 503”. Untick the fault and redrive the <code>OrderPlaced</code> first:
      an <code>OrderPaid</code> redriven before it finds no card, is refused with 409, and can come
      back as a dead letter. The card appears once.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      {SOURCES.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <a href={s.href} rel="noopener">
            {s.title}
          </a>
          , {s.by}, read on {READ}: {s.for}.
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="hint">
      Back to <a href={howUrl('queue')}>the Queue principle on How it’s built</a>, to the decision
      that chose it,{' '}
      <a href={adrUrl('0004')} rel="noopener">
        ADR 0004
      </a>
      , or to{' '}
      <a href={SITE_LAB} rel="noopener">
        the lab
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
